A1. While a few in the subway keep their seats, this is what the majority try to keep. ~ EQUILIBRIUM
A10. This has winning qualities. ~ CUP
A11. A breath of fragrance from Bermuda. ~ ONION
A12. On some of the ocean liners these kind of people do not have very good standing among the other passengers. ~ ILL
A15. This stands for a man and dances for a woman. ~ HE
A16. He who likes to live the outdoor life will always find this one ready to join him. ~ ANT
A17. This is moving along slowly despite the depression. ~ SNAIL
A19. This is over done by the poet. ~ OER
A21. This has carried quite a few people over the Wall Street depression. ~ BUS
A22. The last of the old-fashioned cake of ice. ~ PUDDLE
A23. Make it snappy and mark it rush. ~ RAPID
A25. If you find this one hard, a cup of coffee might help some. ~ BUN
A26. The peanut vender. ~ DAGO
A29. This one always makes a good driver. ~ MIDIRON
A31. What we hope prosperity will do. ~ RECUR
A32. This is the first person that has never been singular in some way or other. ~ WE
A34. One half of a quarter. ~ BIT
A35. This one will undoubtedly appear in person. ~ PER
A36. A carpet sweeper, stocking darner and fireless cooker combined in one. ~ MA
A37. Do not worry about this. It is always in the dictionary. ~ AY
A38. What he did when he said it with ink. ~ BLOTTED
A40. One of "three little words" from Caesar. ~ ET
A41. And here is some old English for you. ~ YE
A42. Don't turn down this street in Paris. ~ RUE
A43. This is permitted in America and also in England. ~ LET
A44. Believe it or not, this is what they said it was. ~ SO
A45. "There is a tide in the affairs of men" and last year this is what it did. ~ EBBED
A47. In order to get in the picture, this is what she does. ~ REDUCES
A51. An expression of dissatisfaction. ~ DRAT
A52. Some people do not seem to like definitions as clear as this. ~ MUD
A53. This one is just plain moony. ~ LUNAR
A55. Well this is what started it all. ~ ORIGIN
A57. A fly swatter. ~ BAT
A58. You will have a hard time untangling this one. ~ WEB
A60. If you want to keep a secret, do not tell it to this one. ~ WORLD
A61. What they should do to those political plums. ~ CAN
A62. Make a note of this and try it on your piano. ~ LA
A63. Here is something new in a prefix. ~ NEO
A64. What Sandy puts on when his kilties are worn out. ~ PATCH
A66. A puzzle racket. ~ DIN
A67. This one has been on top just long enough to have its head completely turned. ~ WEATHERCOCK

D1. The only one that can keep a woman from having the last word. ~ ECHO
D2. This describes "everyone but thee and me." ~ QUEER
D3. It will be hard to get this if you wait until morning. ~ UP
D4. Look (but not stop and listen). ~ LO
D5. Tuned out. ~ INAUDIBLE
D6. This is where a good share of the farmer's crop will go (plural). ~ BINS
D7. Blather. ~ ROT
D8. The voice he let out when he opened up the goods. ~ IN
D9. A woman will probably change this. ~ MIND
D13. Just one round after another. ~ LADDER
D14. Best smeller. ~ LILAC
D17. A wonderful source of delight. ~ SUN
D18. Here is a chance to use that bean of yours (plur.). ~ LEGUMES
D20. A father to his flock. ~ RAM
D21. A bridge tender. ~ BID
D22. To appreciate this you must have a sense of humor. ~ PUN
D24. Anything as easy as this will not bother you. ~ PI
D25. When this was done the Scotch became very tight. ~ BOTTLED
D27. A loud speaker. ~ ORATOR
D28. What the skyscraper did because of the moonshine. ~ SWAYED
D30. A flow of Spanish. ~ RIO
D31. There is a shade of meaning in this that may be hard to get. ~ RED
D33. It will take a lot of pluck to line this up. ~ EYEBROW
D35. Perhaps you will think this is bad humor. ~ PETULANCE
D38. This one made its debut in the spring. ~ BUD
D39. This is short because we didn't wish to make it tedious. ~ TED
D42. Some people do this on the interest of their money, others do it on their backbone. ~ RETIRE
D46. We will try and give you a clear title for this one. ~ BARON
D47. This varies in length and has gone from the knee to the ankle. ~ RUN
D48. Many business firms often give this to their employees. ~ CUT
D49. Two-thirds of ten, one-third of twenty, or two-fifths of seven. ~ EN
D50. With teeth set, this one kept an even temper through it all. ~ SAW
D52. This is as far west as Chicago. ~ MID
D54. A 1929 stock certificate. ~ RELIC
D56. This has always been a shining light. ~ GLOW
D57. The only thing they didn't take when they left the hotel. ~ BATH
D59. We hope you will never see a close-up of this. ~ BANK
D61. Well, who let this out of the bag? ~ CAT
D64. A combination of a cash register, bottle opener and heat regulator. ~ PA
D65. We are just allowing you a short time for this. ~ HR
D66. If you can make this perform, your troubles are over. ~ DO


